      "adopted": false,
      "announce_resolution": false,
      "follow_up_wave": "OPS-ALERT-ADOPT-TREND_MODE_READOUT_DUE-W{NEXT}",
      "envelope_adopted": true,
      "notes": "SIGNAL-TREND-MODE-ENABLE-W1 CH2. ONCE-EVER at flip+30d, behind /var/lib/algovault-monitoring/trend-mode-readout-due.fired. This is the scheduled half of 'the readout is scheduled, not remembered' — a follow-up that depends on someone remembering is prose, not a control. adopted=FALSE because a once-ever due-date alert has no FIRING->CLEAR edge, so --clear on it would be a no-op wearing the look of state hygiene. It STILL names a follow_up_wave: the repo requires every unadopted row to name who will revisit it, and that rule is not mine to argue with from inside a row — the follow-up may well conclude 'not applicable', but the conclusion belongs in that wave rather than in this note."
    },
    {
      "alert_id": "scorer_input_identity",
      "owner": "ops/monitoring/scorer-input-identity-canary.py",
      "hosts": [
        "signal-1"
      ],
      "adopted": true,
      "announce_resolution": false,
      "envelope_adopted": false,
      "envelope_follow_up_wave": "OPS-ENVELOPE-ADOPT-scorer_input_identity-W{NEXT}",
      "notes": "OPS-SCORER-INPUT-PERSISTENCE-W1 R3. Fires when the persisted scorer parts stop reproducing their own total — SUM(bucket_i * WEIGHTS_i) != raw0, or raw0 + the three adjustment deltas != raw_final — on any of the three capture arms, OR when an arm has ZERO captured rows in the window. Those two cases page and nothing else does, and the split is DATA vs INSTRUMENT: a wrong row and a missing row are both unrecoverable, because capture is forward-only, while an unreadable psql is an instrument fault the inventory's own DARK check already covers — paging on it would train the operator to ignore the channel. Pages on the FIRST occurrence rather than behind a consecutive-run confirmation, deliberately: a confirmation window on a forward-only corpus buys certainty by spending the data it is protecting. adopted=true — the canary calls --clear on its healthy path, because send_telegram.sh writes its cooldown marker on a delivered fire and nothing else removes it, so a healed breach would otherwise pin the channel's last word to the worst thing that ever happened. announce_resolution stays FALSE per the law's default: recovery chatter is noise, and the operator action here is a capture fix, not an acknowledgement."
    },
    {
      "alert_id": "disk_headroom",
      "owner": "ops/monitoring/disk-headroom-canary.py",
      "hosts": [
        "signal-1"
      ],
      "adopted": true,
      "announce_resolution": false,
      "envelope_adopted": false,
      "envelope_follow_up_wave": "OPS-ENVELOPE-ADOPT-disk_headroom-W{NEXT}",
      "notes": "OPS-SCORER-INPUT-PERSISTENCE-W1 R4 (Q4-ii). The control that makes this wave's UNBOUNDED retention commitment a gate rather than a promise: no sampling and no TTL on the scorer-input corpus was approved ON CONDITION that headroom is alarmed. Fires at >=85% (schedule a reclaim) and >=92% (reclaim now — Postgres loses WAL/vacuum/temp headroom well before 100%). BOTH tiers deliver, because CRITICAL_PERSISTENT is the only severity send_telegram.sh actually delivers by its own contract; the tier is carried in the body instead of in a severity string the wrapper would drop, and the ladder is still meaningful because the two tiers imply different urgencies of the same operator action. DETECT AND ALERT ONLY — the canary prunes nothing; attribution is ops/scripts/disk-forensics.sh (read-only) and reclaim is a separate Plan-Mode-gated wave, per the never-automate-a-destructive-recovery rule. An unreadable df is INDETERMINATE and SILENT. adopted=true (--clear on the healthy path, so a reclaimed disk does not leave the channel pinned at its worst reading). Scoped to the whole filesystem, not to this wave's tables: measured 2026-08-31 the capture adds ~6.95 MB/day against 238G free, i.e. ~991 months of runway from this wave ALONE, so a scorer-scoped alarm would stay quiet while the box filled from anywhere else. Consumer: OPS-SCORER-INPUT-RETENTION-W{NEXT}, gated on THIS ALERT FIRING, never on a date."
    },
    {
      "alert_id": "regime_budget_starvation",
      "owner": "ops/monitoring/regime-budget-starvation-canary.py",
      "hosts": [
        "signal-1"
      ],
      "adopted": true,
      "announce_resolution": false,
      "envelope_adopted": false,
      "envelope_follow_up_wave": "OPS-ENVELOPE-ADOPT-regime_budget_starvation-W{NEXT}",
      "notes": "OPS-HL-INTERACTIVE-STARVATION-W1 CH3. Fires when interactive BUDGET_CEILING refusals on a venue exceed 1.0 per 100 get_market_regime calls over a trailing 24h. THE METRIC IS NORMALISED PER CALL, NOT A RAW COUNT, and that is the whole design: on 2026-08-28 throws went to EXACTLY ZERO while nothing was fixed — traffic simply fell from ~2,900 calls/day to 581 — so a raw count cannot tell a fix from a demand outage. Below MIN_CALLS (1500) the verdict is INDETERMINATE and never PASS, which is that natural experiment refusing to be read as health a second time; a breach still FAILS at any volume, because throws happening at all is information. This alarm exists because the underlying defect ran from 2026-07-07 and at ~1,000/day from 2026-08-21 on a PAID tool with nothing watching it for ten days — the severity was never the refusal, which is correctly shaped, but its invisibility. DETECT AND ALERT ONLY: it never mutates a budget, a reserve, a ceiling or a network rule, per the never-automate-a-rate-budget rule. An unreadable psql is an instrument fault and is SILENT INDETERMINATE — the inventory's own DARK check already covers a canary that stops producing. Reads as aoe_readonly, NOT algovault_autopilot: measured 2026-08-31, autopilot has no SELECT on rate_limit_events, and a table a role cannot read returns as ABSENT rather than DENIED — a silent zero on exactly the arm that matters. adopted=true (--clear on the healthy path, so a healed breach does not leave the channel pinned to its worst reading); announce_resolution stays FALSE per the law's default — recovery chatter is noise."
    }
  ],
  "_envelope_conformance": "OPS-MONITORING-SIGNAL-CONTRACT-W1 CH2. envelope_adopted = the owner emits a conforming DETECTOR_ENVELOPE (ops/monitoring/detector-envelope.schema.json) and its consumer gates on the envelope token. It is a SEPARATE column from adopted on purpose: adopted answers \"does the owner call --clear on its healthy path\", which is a different question, and one boolean meaning two things is the defect this wave is named after. Default FALSE, and every false row names the wave that will migrate it — no silent exemptions, and the gate is never warn-moded to accommodate one. Unadopted rows behave EXACTLY as before this wave, inheriting _unadopted_behaviour verbatim.",
  "_source_union": "OPS-MONITORING-SIGNAL-CONTRACT-W1 CH2. This registry is now the UNION of two derivations: call-site detection (scripts/check-alert-registry.mjs SHAPES) and monitoring-inventory.json[].alert_ids. Before the union, 14 declared ids were invisible here — including DIRECTIONAL_LABEL_CAPACITY_SHORTFALL, whose false page commissioned this wave; it is raised through a Python subprocess.run([...]) argv list, a shape call-site detection does not match. The fix is deliberately NOT a fourth regex: ALERT_SOURCE_DELTA_VERDICT reports any disagreement between the sources rather than silently resolving to the smaller set.",
  "_envelope_adopted_counts_alerts_not_code_adopters": "OPS-AOE-LIVENESS-W1 CH1, recorded because the two counts diverge and the divergence is STRUCTURAL, not a backlog. `envelope_adopted` is a property of an ALERT ROW, so it can only ever count detectors that page. There are THREE code adopters of DETECTOR_ENVELOPE — src/scripts/backfill-directional-labels.ts (producer), ops/monitoring/labeler_truncation_attribution.py, and monitoring/aoe-host/aoe-output-liveness-canary.py — but only TWO rows can ever carry envelope_adopted:true, because labeler_truncation_attribution.py is DIGEST-ONLY with alert_ids:[] by design (its inventory row: \"must never page, because truncation is safe and self-healing\"). Minting an alert_id for it purely to make a count read 3 would page a detector this estate deliberately silenced — the field is not undercounting, it is counting a different thing. Making \"declared-only by design\" distinguishable from \"declared-only by omission\" is OPS-ALERT-REGISTRY-CROSS-REPO-FLAG-W{NEXT}."
}
